From 60c619f9ecfe01ccde23907b04d92bdb3fb7643e Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: qiyinxi <qiyinxi@foxmail.com>
Date: Sun, 15 Nov 2026 04:48:10 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 105/110] MAS local: give the lean title bar a maximize /
 restore button
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

ModAVD#161 lets the system maximize and snap the lean window, but only a
double click on the title bar, Win+Up, a drag to the top of the screen or
the system menu did it, and nothing showed that it could be done. The title
bar now has a button between minimize and close: the maximize icon and the
tool tip 最大化 while the window is not maximized, the restore icon and 还原
while it is. The button follows the window however it gets there: it asks
the system (IsZoomed) whenever the window's state or size changes. A
snapped window is not maximized and shows maximize, as the system's own
button does. Its accessible name follows the tool tip, so UI Automation
sees which one it is.

A click posts SC_MAXIMIZE or SC_RESTORE, the system command a double click
on the title bar gives, so it goes the same way. Off Windows, where the
lean title bar is not shown, the container falls back on Qt's
showMaximized() / showNormal().

To Windows the button is the window's maximize button: WM_NCHITTEST answers
HTMAXBUTTON over it, and Windows 11 shows its snap layouts there. The mouse
over it is then non-client and Qt gets none of its events, so the window
works out the button's highlight and click from WM_NCMOUSEMOVE (tracking
WM_NCMOUSELEAVE), WM_NCLBUTTONDOWN / WM_NCLBUTTONDBLCLK and WM_NCLBUTTONUP
for HTMAXBUTTON, and none of these go on to DefWindowProc, which would
track the press itself and draw the system's caption button over the title
bar. A click there clicks the widget, so a disabled button still does
nothing; the button shows its tool tip itself after the usual delay. Right
clicks on it are swallowed. Messages for any other part of the window go on
as before. While the button is disabled (the safe shutdown) or hidden,
leanFrameGeometry() passes no range for it and it is an ordinary client
button again.

The arithmetic is in lean-window-frame, tested in
aemu-ui-lean-window_unittests: Hit::MaxButton (HTMAXBUTTON) from hitTest()
for the button's range, which reaches the top edge like the other buttons
but leaves the corner and right edge bands alone; maxButtonFace() and
maxButtonCommand() for the state; MaxButtonTracker for the non-client
mouse messages, where a press that leaves the button is given up, since
the mouse is not captured.

Refs ModAVD/ModAVD#185

